  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:  \nWe represent the step function \\( h(x) \\) using two intervals on \\([0, 2]\\) that cover a total length of \\(1 / \\text{dx}\\) (where \\(\\text{dx} = 2.0 / 606\\)), ensuring a total integral of 1. This reduces the optimization space from 606 dimensions to three variables (length of the first interval, starting position of the first interval, and gap between the two intervals). The goal is to maximize the minimum overlap integral \\( F_k = \\int h(x) \\cdot h(x+k) \\, dx \\) over shifts \\(k\\). Minimizing the maximum \\( f_k = \\int h(x) \\cdot (1 - h(x+k)) \\, dx = 1 - F_k \\) is then achieved by this optimization. This method is more robust than the previous multi-phase Adam/SLSQP optimization because it explicitly targets the minimal maximum overlap and leverages the piecewise constant structure, which is known to produce high-quality solutions for similar problems.\n\nMathematical Specification:  \nWe define:  \n- \\( I1 \\): An interval \\([a, b]\\) with length \\(L1\\).  \n- \\( I2 \\): An interval \\([c, d]\\) with length \\(L2 = 303 - L1\\) (since \\(303 \\times \\text{dx} = 1\\)).  \n\nThe constraints are:  \n1. \\( 0 \\leq L1 \\leq 303 \\): The total length must be exactly \\(303\\) grid points wide.  \n2. \\( 0 \\leq a \\leq 603 - L1 \\): Ensures \\(I1\\)( and \\(I2\\) fit within \\([0, 605]\\).  \n3. \\( c \\geq a + b \\): Ensures disjointness.  \n\nThe optimization variable is: \\(x = [L1, a, c]\\).  \n\nThe objective is to minimize \\( f_x = 1 - \\min_k F_k \\), where \\( F_k \\) is the overlap integral computed as the length of the support intersection up to \\(605\\) grid points.  \n\nThe search space is defined by bounding \\(L1, a, c\\) and enforcing constraints via projection.  \n\nImplementation Procedure:  \n1. `run(seed=42, budget_s=1300, **kwargs)` initializes with \\( x = [303 / 2, 100, 200] \\) (demonstrates but not run due to time).  \n\nThe optimization steps are:  \n1. Compute bounds and constraints for \\( L1, a, c \\).  \n2. Use `minimize` with SLSQP to find the variable \\( x \\).  \n3. After optimization, discretize the intervals to grid points and ensure conservative feasibility.  \n4. If \\( F \\min \\) improves the record, return the candidate.  \n\nKey operations:  \n- `compute_objective(x)`: Computes \\( \\min_k F_k \\) for given \\( x \\).  \n- `create_grid(x)`: Converts continuous intervals to grid-aligned \\( h \\).  \n- `transform_x_to_interval(x)`: Converts \\( x \\) to interval parameters.  \n  - `feasibility_check(a, b)`: Projects \\(a, b\\) to satisfy constraints.  \n  - `project_to_final(x)`: Finalizes \\( x \\) to ensure sum is \\(303\\).  \n\nSearch Choices:  \n- Uses \\( L1, a, c \\) as primary variables.  \n- Iteration: 50.  \n- Initial length: 303/2 = 151.5.  \n\nComputebudget and Robustness:  \n- Dominant cost: Projecting \\( x \\) and computing the min overlap for each evaluation.  \n- Time check within loops.  \n- Automatic fallback to finished candidate.  \n\nValidation and Return Structure:  \n1. Feasibility and finite value checks.  \n2. Re-evaluate the candidate using actual measures.  \n3. Return \\((h, \\text{score}, 606)\\) if better than record.  \n\nFinal Structure:  \n- Optimize three variables.  \n- Convert to grid.  \n- Return best results.",
